## Solution

We solve
$$\frac{d^{2}w}{dz^{2}}=\left(\frac{dw}{dz}\right)^{2}\frac{2w-1}{w^{2}+1}.$$

**Reduction of order.** Set $p=\dfrac{dw}{dz}$. By the chain rule,
$$\frac{d^{2}w}{dz^{2}}=p\,\frac{dp}{dw}.$$

The equation becomes
$$p\,\frac{dp}{dw}=p^{2}\,\frac{2w-1}{w^{2}+1}.$$

**Case $p\neq 0$.** Dividing by $p$ gives a separable equation:
$$\frac{dp}{p}=\frac{2w-1}{w^{2}+1}\,dw.$$

Integrating both sides,
$$\ln|p|=\int\frac{2w}{w^{2}+1}\,dw-\int\frac{1}{w^{2}+1}\,dw=\ln(w^{2}+1)-\arctan w+C.$$

Hence
$$p=\frac{dw}{dz}=A\,(w^{2}+1)\,e^{-\arctan w},\qquad A\neq 0.$$

Separating again,
$$dz=\frac{e^{\arctan w}}{A\,(w^{2}+1)}\,dw.$$

With the substitution $u=\arctan w$, $du=\dfrac{dw}{w^{2}+1}$, the right–hand side integrates to
$$z=\frac{1}{A}\,e^{\arctan w}+B.$$

Solving for $w$:
$$e^{\arctan w}=A(z-B)\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;\arctan w=\ln\!\big(A(z-B)\big),$$

so, renaming constants $C_{1}=A$, $C_{2}=-AB$,
$$\boxed{\,w=\tan\!\big(\ln(C_{1}z+C_{2})\big)\,},\qquad C_{1}\neq 0.$$

**Case $p=0$.** Then $w\equiv w_{0}$ (constant), and both sides vanish identically, so every constant function is also a (singular) solution.

**Verification of the non‑constant family.** Let $t=\ln(C_{1}z+C_{2})$, so $w=\tan t$ and $\dfrac{dt}{dz}=\dfrac{C_{1}}{C_{1}z+C_{2}}=C_{1}e^{-t}$. Then
$$\frac{dw}{dz}=\sec^{2}t\cdot C_{1}e^{-t},\qquad
\frac{d^{2}w}{dz^{2}}=C_{1}^{2}e^{-2t}\sec^{2}t\,(2\tan t-1).$$

On the other hand,
$$\left(\frac{dw}{dz}\right)^{2}\frac{2w-1}{w^{2}+1}
=C_{1}^{2}\sec^{4}t\,e^{-2t}\cdot\frac{2\tan t-1}{\sec^{2}t}
=C_{1}^{2}e^{-2t}\sec^{2}t\,(2\tan t-1),$$

which agrees with $\dfrac{d^{2}w}{dz^{2}}$. ✓

**Answer.** The general (non‑constant) solution is
$$w(z)=\tan\!\big(\ln(C_{1}z+C_{2})\big),\qquad C_{1}\neq 0,\; C_{1}z+C_{2}>0,$$
together with the singular constant solutions $w\equiv w_{0}$.

